{
  "_note": "Smallmouth bass (Micropterus dolomieu), an uncommon fighter of rocky Midwest lakes. Markings from field-guide descriptions: bronze to brown-olive, with faint dark vertical bars on the sides, dark bars radiating back from a red eye across the cheek, a joined dorsal fin with a shallow notch, and a jaw that ends below the eye. From above, as seen from a boat: a chunky torpedo about a fifth as wide as it is long, widest a third of the way back just behind the gill covers, the head tapering smoothly to a rounded point. The dorsal and anal fins are thin wedges and the tail a thin blade, as on a real fish, so the fins read from the side and the tail fans out as it swims (SECOND-ORDER R2.130-R2.139). 350 mm long, the base length (SECOND-ORDER R1.150-R1.169).",
